---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import HunterPlay from '../../components/HunterPlay.astro';
const b = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const sc=[['Survivor','careful','180','survived 30 days','30.0','29.7','360.2'],['Starvation','careful','49','died of hunger','-2.0','-2.6','90.24'],['Predator','reckless','12','killed by predator','-9.0','-9.6','24.68'],['Skilled hunter','big game','180','survived 30 days','30.0','29.9','432.51'],['Hunt injury','stubborn','15','died on big hunt','-8.0','-8.6','26.12']];
---
<Base title="Hunter-Gatherer" description="A custom finite MDP for CENG567 Reinforcement Learning, playable in the browser.">
<div class="wrap pagehead"><span class="tag live">Ongoing · CENG567, Dr. Alper Demir</span><h1 >Hunter-Gatherer Survival</h1><p class="lead">A finite MDP and Gymnasium environment I designed for <a href="https://ceng.iyte.edu.tr/?p=11940">Dr. Alper Demir</a>'s graduate reinforcement learning course. Survive 30 days. The game below runs the same rules in your browser.</p></div>
<div class="wrap grid2">
 <div class="rv"><HunterPlay /></div>
 <div class="prose rv"><h2 class="h2">Trade-offs</h2><ul class="dl"><li><b>Fruit or hunting:</b> fruit is safe and limited, and hunting is unlimited and uncertain.</li><li><b>Small or big hunt:</b> a big hunt gives twice the energy and can injure you. Its odds only improve by succeeding at it.</li><li><b>Night:</b> sleeping in camp is free. Outside you lose extra energy and risk a predator.</li><li><b>Injury:</b> a second injury kills. Healing takes rest while energy keeps draining.</li></ul>
 <div class="stats"><div><b data-count="8640">8640</b><span>observation states: energy, health, phase, location, fruit, skill</span></div><div><b data-count="7">7</b><span>actions</span></div><div><b data-count="3">3</b><span>reward functions compared</span></div></div></div>
</div>
<div class="wrap section"><h2 class="h2 rv">Reward design</h2><div class="tw rv"><table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Policy</th><th>Steps</th><th>Outcome</th><th>Sparse</th><th>Dense</th><th>Log</th></tr></thead><tbody>{sc.map(r=><tr>{r.map(v=><td>{v}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="prose rv" style="margin-top:14px">The log column shows why reward design matters: it gives the starving agent +90.24 even though it dies, because every step alive pays. The first two rows use the same policy, so the difference between them is pure environment randomness.</p></div>
<div class="wrap section grid2"><figure class="rv" style="margin:0"><video src={`${b}/media/research/hg_3_predator.webm`} autoplay muted loop playsinline style="width:100%;border:2px solid var(--wall)"></video><figcaption class="note">Scenario 3: never returns to camp and dies to a predator on the second night.</figcaption></figure><figure class="rv" style="margin:0"><img src={`${b}/media/research/hg_energy.png`} alt="Energy over time for five scenarios" style="width:100%;border:2px solid var(--wall);background:#fff" loading="lazy"><figcaption class="note">Energy over time for all five scenarios. Grey marks night, orange marks injury.</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="wrap section"><hr class="rule"><div class="prose rv" style="margin-top:28px"><h2 class="h2">What comes next</h2><p>The environment grows through the course, and I am extending it to several agents who share the forest and hunt together. Those experiments are in progress, and I will add them here once they are ready.</p></div></div>
</Base>
<style is:global>.dl{padding-left:18px;margin:0}.dl li{margin-block:6px}.hbtn button{font-size:12px;padding:6px 8px}</style>
